# sublimapparel.com — Cloudflare Pages redirects
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
#
# ============================================================================
# LLM-friendly files removed from the public root (2026-09-14 R64 follow-up).
#
# Why: Cloudflare Pages' build-time auto-robots scanner sees any file whose
# path matches `/llms*.txt` in the build output and auto-injects
# `Sitemap: https://<host>/llms.txt` plus a Yandex `Host:` directive into the
# served /robots.txt. We confirmed this on 2026-09-14: the live
# https://sublimapparel.com/robots.txt after the previous build returned the
# local fine-grained AI-bot Allow / Disallow blocks followed by
# `Host: https://sublimapparel.com` and `Sitemap: https://sublimapparel.com/llms.txt`
# even though neither line was in the source file. Even with no
# `Content-Signal:` directive present, CF still augments.
#
# The cleanest fix: drop the /llms.txt and /llms-full.txt URLs entirely.
# LLM discoverability of the AEO content is preserved at the HTML layer via
# the per-page `<link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" href="<page>.html.md">`
# tags + the per-page `Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"` HTTP headers
# (we keep the latter only for the per-page rules in _headers, even though
# /llms.txt itself now 404s — this is intentional: crawlers will silently
# skip the missing file but still see the markdown alternates). The
# Markdown-converted AEO content itself moves to /aep/<page>.html.md and
# is served as a real file. If we ever re-enable /llms.txt we must turn
# off the auto-robots feature in the Cloudflare Pages dashboard.
# ============================================================================
# (llms.txt 200 rewrites removed; the actual files are also deleted from
#  public/llms/ in the same commit to prevent the build-time scanner from
#  matching the file path. See backup tag pre-r64-rss-redirect-2026-09-14.)
#
# ============================================================================
# Blog RSS feed — canonical URL is /blog/feed.xml (no trailing slash) so
# the blog page's <link rel="alternate"> and the feed's own <atom:link
# rel="self"> can both reference the same path.
#
# IMPORTANT: Next.js `output: "export"` cannot produce a `index.xml` file
# via `route.ts` (any URL path containing a dot extension is silently
# skipped). We pre-render the RSS body to public/blog/feed.xml/index.xml
# at build time via scripts/build-rss.mjs, then `next build` copies
# public/ → out/ verbatim so the file lands at
# out/blog/feed.xml/index.xml.
#
# Cloudflare Pages by default only recognizes `index.html` as a directory
# index file — `index.xml` is NOT auto-served when the request is
# `/blog/feed.xml/`. Without help, `/blog/feed.xml/` 404s (CF falls back
# to the project 404 page) and only `/blog/feed.xml/index.xml` directly
# works. We fix this with two 200 internal rewrites below so both the
# canonical no-slash URL and the with-slash URL serve the same file.
# The Content-Type override (`application/rss+xml; charset=utf-8`) is in
# _headers and applies to both request paths via the `/blog/feed.xml/*`
# header rule. The 200 status means CF internally rewrites and returns
# 200 to the client (no client-visible 301 hop). RSS aggregators see a
# single clean 200 response with the proper Content-Type.
# ============================================================================
/blog/feed.xml            /blog/feed.xml/index.xml       200
/blog/feed.xml/*          /blog/feed.xml/index.xml       200
